  "notes": "Korean idol pop built from hip-hop, EDM and disco modes with a seven-voice arrangement, flagged in the research as a K-pop sub-lane. The Lane line names the hip-hop mode first and gives it the half-time class — \"the hip-hop mode is half-time (backbeat on 3) and the disco/EDM modes are 2&4 straight four-on-the-floor\" — so `halfTime` is true at `halftime_3`, and the disco mode survives as `fourOnFloorInChorus` rather than as a second placement. Because the placement matches the parent, the separation comes from the kit, which the entry gives era by era: \"1990s boom-bap kit on the debut material, then trap kick/808-and-roll patterns, then moombahton and tropical-house percussion, then a disco four-square kick with handclaps. Group-chant percussion hits mark choreography accents.\" That is a wide `kick.densityPerBar`, a high roll `freqPerBar` with a 32nd vocabulary, and a perc list carrying `conga`, `snap`, `clap`-adjacent lanes at high density for the chant hits. The 808 is authored because the entry names it — \"808 sub with slides in the trap mode; funk electric bass in the disco mode; filtered pumping sub in the EDM mode\" — as `counter_riff`, so the funk/pumping bass keeps generating alongside it. The arrangement block is what the research calls the transferable parameter: 8-bar sections throughout, and the highest `switchUpProb` weighting for the documented \"beat/genre switch before the final chorus\". The entry's \"dance-break instrumental section\" is carried by the `bridge` slot, which both structures use — engine/src/arrange.rs has no dance-break section kind and nothing strips a section to instruments, so this is the closest available encoding rather than a literal one. The rap-line/vocal-line split is the widest `densityPerBar` here; \"hook-first with a chanted group refrain\" is `call_response` with `endVariation`. ⚠ `confidence` is medium and the research says \"tempo/harmony bands are genre-derived\": every drum probability, the humanize numbers, the harmonic weights, the melody register and `bpm.mode` 120 (the midpoint of the stated 90—150 band) are INFERRED, and the entry's [LOW-CONF] mark on the rap/vocal-split claim is repeated here.",
